{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"prototypes","title":"Prototypes & the Prototype Chain","group":"Prototypes & Classes","order":30,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">JavaScript objects inherit directly from other objects. Every object has a hidden link, <code>[[Prototype]]</code>, and property lookups walk that chain until they find a match or hit <code>null</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Prototype</strong> — the object another object delegates to for properties it does not have itself. Read it with <code>Object.getPrototypeOf(obj)</code> (or the legacy <code>obj.__proto__</code>).</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i>Lookup walks the chain</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const animal = { eats: true, describe() { return `${this.name} eats: ${this.eats}`; } };\nconst rabbit = Object.create(animal);\nrabbit.name = \"Bunny\";\nrabbit.jumps = true;\n\nconsole.log(rabbit.jumps, rabbit.eats, rabbit.describe());\nconsole.log(Object.hasOwn(rabbit, \"eats\"), \"eats\" in rabbit);\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(rabbit) === animal);\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(animal) === Object.prototype);\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(Object.prototype));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>true true Bunny eats: true\nfalse true\ntrue\ntrue\nnull</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>rabbit</code></div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>animal</code></div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>Object.prototype</code></div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>null</code></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-pencil\"></i>Writes do not walk the chain</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const proto = { greeting: \"hello\", list: [] };\nconst child = Object.create(proto);\nchild.greeting = \"hi\";           // creates an OWN property (shadowing)\nchild.list.push(1);              // mutates the SHARED array on the prototype\nconsole.log(child.greeting, proto.greeting, proto.list);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>hi hello [ 1 ]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-exchange\"></i><code>prototype</code> vs <code>[[Prototype]]</code></h3>\n<p>Two different things with confusingly similar names:</p>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-function\"></i><code>Fn.prototype</code></h3><p>A regular property that exists on functions (and classes). It becomes the <code>[[Prototype]]</code> of objects created with <code>new Fn()</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i><code>[[Prototype]]</code> / <code>__proto__</code></h3><p>The internal link every object has, pointing to the object it inherits from.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function Dog(name) { this.name = name; }\nDog.prototype.bark = function () { return `${this.name}: woof`; };\n\nconst rex = new Dog(\"Rex\");\nconsole.log(rex.bark());\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(rex) === Dog.prototype);\nconsole.log(Dog.prototype.constructor === Dog, rex.constructor.name);\nconsole.log(Object.hasOwn(rex, \"bark\"), rex instanceof Dog);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Rex: woof\ntrue\ntrue Dog\nfalse true</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Methods live <strong>once</strong> on the prototype and are shared by every instance — cheaper than giving each instance its own copy.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sitemap\"></i>Built-in chains</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const arr = [1, 2];\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(arr) === Array.prototype);\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(Array.prototype) === Object.prototype);\nconst fn = () =&gt; {};\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(fn) === Function.prototype);\nconsole.log(arr.hasOwnProperty(\"length\"), arr.hasOwnProperty(\"map\"));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>true\ntrue\ntrue\ntrue false</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>How instanceof works</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function myInstanceOf(obj, Ctor) {\n  let proto = Object.getPrototypeOf(obj);\n  while (proto !== null) {\n    if (proto === Ctor.prototype) return true;\n    proto = Object.getPrototypeOf(proto);\n  }\n  return false;\n}\nconsole.log(myInstanceOf([], Array), myInstanceOf([], Object), myInstanceOf([], Map));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>true true false</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Never extend built-in prototypes (<code>Array.prototype.myThing = …</code>) in shared code — it can clash with future standard methods (this is why <code>Array.prototype.flatten</code> had to be renamed <code>flat</code>).</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Explain prototypal inheritance.</summary><div><p>Each object has an internal <code>[[Prototype]]</code> link to another object. When a property is not found on the object, the engine looks it up on the prototype, then that object's prototype, and so on until <code>null</code>. Methods are shared this way; classes are syntax over the same mechanism.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between __proto__ and prototype?</summary><div><p><code>__proto__</code> (really <code>[[Prototype]]</code>) is the link on every object to what it inherits from. <code>prototype</code> is a property on constructor functions/classes: the object that becomes the <code>[[Prototype]]</code> of instances created with <code>new</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}